\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{corl_2026}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,graphicx,booktabs,xcolor,hyperref,microtype}
\usepackage[normalem]{ulem}

\newcommand{\placeholder}[1]{\textcolor{red}{[#1]}}
\newcommand{\ours}{GeoSafe}

\title{GeoSafe: Internalizing Collision Geometry into Flow-Matching VLA Weights for Zero-Overhead Safe Manipulation}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have advanced robot manipulation toward general-purpose deployment, yet collision safety remains an unresolved challenge for real-world use. Most existing safety methods operate at inference time, adding computational overhead; training-time alternatives based on reinforcement learning reduce violations but lack geometric grounding for precise spatial collision reasoning.

We propose \ours{}, a training-time approach that embeds collision awareness directly into flow-matching VLA weights. During fine-tuning, a lightweight auxiliary head predicts per-timestep Signed Distance Field (SDF) values from the model's internal representations, and a collision-shaping loss penalizes trajectories that violate a safety margin. A noise-aware weighting function $w(t)$ suppresses this penalty at high noise levels where geometric predictions are unreliable, activating it only in the low-noise regime where action predictions carry meaningful spatial structure. After training, the SDF head is discarded entirely: the model runs through the standard inference pipeline with zero additional latency, zero additional memory, and no access to scene geometry.

We evaluate \ours{} on the SafeLIBERO benchmark (32 collision scenarios across 4 task suites), measuring Task Success Rate (TSR) and Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR). \ours{} achieves \placeholder{XX.X}\% TSR and \placeholder{XX.X}\% CAR, compared to \placeholder{XX.X}\% TSR / \placeholder{XX.X}\% CAR for vanilla $\pi_{0.5}$ and \placeholder{XX.X}\% TSR / \placeholder{XX.X}\% CAR for the strongest inference-time baseline. Our approach establishes a connection between guidance distillation in image generation and safety internalization in robot control, providing a ``train-with, infer-without'' framework for embedding geometric safety constraints into generative action policies.
\end{abstract}

%=============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
%=============================================================================

Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as a powerful paradigm for general-purpose robot manipulation. $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi0} demonstrated that a flow-matching architecture built on a pretrained vision-language model can master diverse manipulation tasks across multiple robot platforms, and its successor $\pi_{0.5}$ \citep{black2025pi05} extended this capability to open-world generalization through co-training on heterogeneous data. These models generate smooth, language-conditioned action trajectories by learning velocity fields that transport noise distributions to action distributions, enabling flexible deployment without task-specific engineering. Yet a critical gap separates laboratory success from real-world deployment: collision safety. When a robot executes a manipulation task in a cluttered environment, unintended contact with surrounding objects or the workspace boundary can damage both the robot and the environment, and create safety hazards for nearby humans. The problem is especially acute for VLA models, which generate entire action chunks in a single forward pass without explicit geometric reasoning about obstacle proximity.

Existing approaches to VLA collision safety operate primarily at inference time, introducing computational overhead that conflicts with the real-time demands of robot control. OmniGuide \citep{song2026omniguide} applies differentiable Signed Distance Field (SDF) energy to guide flow-matching VLAs through backpropagation into the denoising loop, improving collision avoidance rates from 7.0\% to 93.5\% on RoboCasa but at approximately $2\times$ inference latency. AEGIS/VLSA \citep{hu2025aegis} projects VLA actions into control barrier function (CBF) certified safe regions as a post-hoc correction layer, achieving the strongest results on SafeLIBERO (68.13\% task success rate, 77.85\% collision avoidance rate on $\pi_{0.5}$). The AEGIS authors, however, explicitly acknowledge that such corrections ``may adjust the trajectory to unfamiliar regions that are statistically rare or absent in the training data of the base VLA model,'' leading to erratic downstream behavior. SafeVLA \citep{zhang2025safevla} takes a training-time approach through constrained reinforcement learning, reducing cumulative safety cost by 83.58\%, but relies on abstract cost functions without geometric grounding for spatial collision reasoning. SafeDiffuser \citep{xiao2025safediffuser} embeds CBF invariance directly into the diffusion denoising procedure, but retains the constraint computation at inference. A fundamental tension runs through these methods: inference-time safety interventions add latency and risk pushing trajectories out of distribution, while training-time approaches based on reinforcement learning lack the geometric structure to reason precisely about collision boundaries.

We draw on an observation from image generation: guidance distillation \citep{meng2023distillation} has shown that inference-time guidance signals can be internalized into model weights during training, producing high-fidelity outputs without runtime guidance computation. We transfer this principle to VLA safety. Instead of distilling classifier-free guidance for image quality, we internalize SDF collision geometry into flow-matching velocity fields for safe action generation. The flow-matching training process provides a natural mechanism for this internalization. Each training sample is associated with a noise level $t \in [0, 1]$ that interpolates between the clean action trajectory and pure noise. At low noise levels, the model's internal representations encode meaningful geometric structure about the predicted trajectory. An SDF auxiliary head, attached to these representations, provides a differentiable signal that measures proximity to collision boundaries. By penalizing collision-prone predictions through this head and weighting the penalty with a noise-dependent function that activates only when geometric predictions are reliable, we shape the learned velocity field to route around obstacles without corrupting the training signal at high noise levels.

We propose \ours{}, a training-time method that embeds collision awareness into a flow-matching VLA through SDF auxiliary supervision and a noise-aware collision-shaping loss. A lightweight two-layer network is attached to the action expert's internal features in $\pi_{0.5}$, predicting per-timestep minimum signed distances between the robot's end-effector and scene obstacles. Ground-truth SDF labels are precomputed offline from the simulation physics engine. The collision-shaping loss penalizes predicted trajectories that violate a safety margin $\delta$, weighted by a sigmoid function $w(t) = \sigma(\alpha(t_0 - t))$ that suppresses the penalty at high noise levels and activates it in the low-noise regime where geometric structure is meaningful. Both the SDF regression loss and the collision penalty propagate gradients through the shared backbone, directly modifying the velocity field that governs action generation. At inference time, the SDF head is discarded entirely: the trained model runs through the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ pipeline with zero additional latency, zero additional memory, and no access to scene geometry. Safety becomes an intrinsic property of the modified weights, not an external constraint imposed at runtime.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We present the first method that internalizes SDF collision geometry into VLA flow-matching weights during training, producing a model that generates inherently collision-aware actions with zero inference overhead. The SDF auxiliary head is dropped at deployment, and the standard inference pipeline runs unchanged (Section~\ref{sec:sdf_head}, \ref{sec:inference}).
\item We propose a noise-aware collision-shaping loss with a time-dependent weight function $w(t)$ that adapts to the flow-matching noise schedule, activating collision penalties only in the low-noise regime where the model's geometric predictions carry reliable spatial information. This prevents noisy gradients at high noise levels from destabilizing training (Section~\ref{sec:loss}).
\item Comprehensive evaluation on SafeLIBERO benchmark comparing with inference-time safety method (AEGIS) and controlled baseline (vanilla $\pi_{0.5}$), demonstrating \placeholder{XX}\% improvement in collision avoidance with negligible task performance degradation (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\item We establish a conceptual bridge between guidance distillation in image generation and safety internalization in VLA, providing a ``train-with, infer-without'' framework for embedding geometric safety constraints into generative robot policies (Section~\ref{sec:inference}).
\end{itemize}


%=============================================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
%=============================================================================

\paragraph{Safe Robot Learning.}
Ensuring collision-free behavior in learned robot policies has been approached primarily through inference-time interventions.
One category embeds safety constraints directly into generative processes: SafeDiffuser \citep{xiao2025safediffuser} incorporates CBF invariance into each diffusion denoising step, providing adaptable safety---constraints can be swapped by changing the CBF without retraining---at the cost of per-step barrier computation that scales with the denoising horizon, while OmniGuide \citep{song2026omniguide} applies differentiable SDF-based energy fields to guide flow-matching VLAs, improving collision avoidance from 7.0\% to 93.5\% CAR on RoboCasa at the cost of approximately $2\times$ inference latency.
A second category applies reactive post-hoc corrections: AEGIS/VLSA \citep{hu2025aegis} projects VLA actions into CBF-certified safe regions, achieving strong results on SafeLIBERO (68.13\% TSR, 77.85\% CAR on $\pi_{0.5}$), but such generate-then-correct pipelines produce jerky trajectories from hard constraint projections and risk compounding errors as corrected actions push the policy into out-of-distribution states where subsequent predictions degrade---a failure mode the authors themselves acknowledge. These reactive approaches offer robustness to novel obstacles through real-time geometric checks, but at the cost of latency and trajectory naturalness that proactive internalization avoids.
Training-time approaches have also been explored, primarily through constrained RL: building on state-wise constrained policy optimization foundations \citep[SCPO;][]{zhao2024scpo}, SafeVLA \citep{zhang2025safevla} reduces cumulative safety cost by 83.58\% via constrained policy optimization. While constrained RL can encode multi-step safety objectives that single-step geometric supervision cannot, these methods require reward shaping and iterative policy-constraint optimization loops that add substantial training complexity, and their abstract cost functions lack the geometric grounding for precise spatial collision reasoning.
Across these paradigms, a tension persists: inference-time methods add latency and risk OOD drift or local minima in dense clutter, while training-time RL methods lack the geometric structure for precise spatial collision reasoning.
\ours{} resolves this tension by internalizing SDF collision geometry into VLA weights during training, producing inherently safe actions with zero inference overhead.

\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}
The development of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models has progressed from diffusion-based action generation to flow-matching architectures with broad generalization.
$\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi0} introduced a flow-matching VLA built on PaliGemma, pre-trained across eight robot platforms on over 10,000 hours of demonstration data, and established the foundation for general-purpose robot control via flow matching.
Its successor $\pi_{0.5}$ \citep{black2025pi05} extends this framework to open-world generalization through co-training on heterogeneous tasks, with open-source weights and fine-tuning infrastructure via the openpi codebase.
Recent work has explored incorporating geometric priors into these architectures.
GLaD \citep{guo2025glad} distills 3D geometric features from a frozen geometry-aware vision transformer into VLA hidden states during pretraining, achieving 94.1\% average success on LIBERO by aligning geometric information with deeper model layers rather than the vision encoder alone.
This demonstrates that geometric distillation can substantially improve VLA spatial reasoning, but GLaD targets general spatial understanding rather than collision safety, with no SDF or collision-specific supervision.
\ours{} builds on the $\pi_{0.5}$ architecture and extends geometric internalization specifically to collision awareness through SDF-based training signals.

\paragraph{Signed Distance Fields in Robotics.}
Signed distance fields (SDF) and related implicit geometric representations have been widely adopted in robot manipulation and motion planning.
In traditional planning, CSSDF-Net \citep{chen2026cssdf} models continuous differentiable SDF directly in configuration space for collision-free trajectory optimization, and NGDF \citep{weng2023ngdf} represents grasps as neural distance fields whose gradients enable joint collision avoidance and trajectory smoothness, with 63\% higher execution success than baselines.
More recently, several VLA works have adopted the ``train-with-geometry, infer-without'' paradigm.
OccVLA \citep{liu2025occvla} uses 3D occupancy prediction as auxiliary supervision for autonomous driving VLAs that can be skipped at inference; Spatial Forcing \citep{li2025spatial} aligns VLA intermediate representations with 3D foundation model features to improve spatial precision without inference cost; and GeoPredict \citep{qian2025geopredict} applies 3D Gaussian geometry supervision dropped at inference to enhance manipulation accuracy.
These works validate that geometric training signals improve VLA spatial capabilities at zero inference cost, but none applies this paradigm to collision safety.
\ours{} specializes the ``train-with, infer-without'' approach to collision avoidance: rather than general spatial alignment, we internalize configuration-space SDF into the policy network through an auxiliary loss that directly shapes flow-matching velocity fields away from obstacle boundaries.

\paragraph{Guidance Distillation.}
The concept of internalizing inference-time guidance into model weights originates in image generation.
\citet{meng2023distillation} showed that classifier-free guidance in diffusion models can be distilled through a two-step process, enabling high-fidelity generation in 1--4 steps with $10\times$ acceleration, and proved that guidance can reside in weights rather than requiring runtime computation.
\ours{} adapts this paradigm to VLA safety: instead of distilling classifier-free guidance for image quality, we internalize SDF collision guidance into flow-matching velocity fields for safe action generation.
Rather than distilling guided sampling trajectories into a student network, we directly shape the velocity field through an SDF auxiliary head whose gradients penalize collision-prone flow vectors during training, and drop this head entirely at inference.

\paragraph{Positioning.}
\ours{} occupies a unique position at the intersection of training-time safety internalization and zero-overhead inference: it is the only approach that combines collision-specific geometric supervision (configuration-space SDF) with full internalization into VLA weights, producing inherently safe actions without runtime safety computation, post-hoc correction, or out-of-distribution drift.


%=============================================================================
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
%=============================================================================

We present \ours{}, a training-time approach that embeds geometric safety awareness into a flow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) model without incurring any additional cost at inference. The key idea is to attach a lightweight auxiliary head that predicts signed distance field (SDF) values from the model's internal action representations, and to train the entire system with a noise-aware collision-shaping loss that penalizes trajectories entering collision regions. After training, the auxiliary head is discarded, and the base policy is deployed unchanged. We build on $\pi_{0.5}$, a state-of-the-art flow-matching VLA, and describe each component below.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Flow-matching action generation.} $\pi_{0.5}$ generates robot action trajectories through conditional flow matching. Given an observation $o$ (RGB images and a language instruction), the model learns a velocity field $v_\theta(x_t, o, t)$ that transports samples from a noise distribution to the data distribution of action chunks. Let $a \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D_a}$ denote a ground-truth action chunk of horizon $H$ and dimension $D_a$, and let $\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ be Gaussian noise. The noisy interpolant at time $t \in [0, 1]$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
x_t = (1 - t) \, a + t \, \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $t = 0$ corresponds to the clean action and $t = 1$ to pure noise. The conditional velocity field target is $u_t = \epsilon - a$, and the flow-matching objective minimizes
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}} = \mathbb{E}_{t, a, \epsilon} \left[ \left\| v_\theta(x_t, o, t) - (\epsilon - a) \right\|^2 \right].
\end{equation}
At inference, the model generates actions by integrating the learned velocity field from $t = 1$ (noise) to $t = 0$ (data) using an ODE solver.

\paragraph{Safety as collision avoidance.} We formalize safety through the signed distance field between the robot's end-effector and scene obstacles. For a robot configuration $q$ in a scene with obstacle set $\mathcal{O}$, the signed distance $s(q) = \min_{o \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{SDF}(q, o)$ is positive when the end-effector is separated from all obstacles and negative when penetration occurs. A trajectory is considered safe if $s(q_t) \geq \delta$ for all timesteps, where $\delta > 0$ is a safety margin. Our goal is to train a policy that intrinsically generates trajectories satisfying this constraint, without requiring runtime collision checking or trajectory filtering.

\paragraph{Core insight.} Rather than imposing safety through constrained optimization at inference (which adds latency and may degrade task performance), we shape the flow-matching training objective so that the learned velocity field steers action trajectories away from collision regions. The SDF auxiliary head acts as a geometric probe during training: it reads the model's internal representation and provides a differentiable safety signal. When gradients from the collision-shaping loss propagate back through this probe into the action generation backbone, the model learns to associate its internal representations with collision-free trajectories.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Head}
\label{sec:sdf_head}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Architecture.} The SDF head is a two-layer multilayer perceptron (MLP) that maps the action expert's internal features to per-timestep signed distance predictions. In $\pi_{0.5}$, the action expert is a Gemma-300M transformer that processes noisy action tokens alongside vision-language context. Let $f_t \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H \times D}$ denote the action expert's output features (with $D = 1024$ for the Gemma-300M variant), which are normally projected to action-space dimension $D_a = 32$ by a linear output layer. We attach the SDF head to these features via a forward hook on the output projection layer, requiring no modification to the base model's source code. The head is defined as
\begin{equation}
\hat{s} = W_2 \, \mathrm{ReLU}(W_1 \, f_t + b_1) + b_2,
\end{equation}
where $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{256 \times 1024}$, $W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 256}$, and $\hat{s} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H}$ gives the predicted minimum signed distance for each timestep in the action chunk. The head contains approximately 263K parameters, which is negligible compared to the 300M-parameter action expert.

\paragraph{Ground-truth SDF computation.} We precompute per-timestep signed distance labels offline from the demonstration dataset using MuJoCo's analytical geometry engine. For each demonstration timestep, we replay the recorded robot state and compute the minimum signed distance between end-effector collision geometries (gripper fingers and the last two arm links) and all scene object geometries, using \texttt{mj\_geomDistance} for exact signed distance queries. Objects currently grasped by the robot are excluded from distance computation to avoid penalizing intentional contact.\footnote{Grasp is detected when the gripper aperture falls below 35\,mm and the minimum finger-to-object distance is less than 5\,mm. See Appendix for implementation details.}

The resulting per-timestep distances are stored as compressed NumPy arrays indexed by task, demonstration, and timestep. At training time, the \texttt{SDFLabelStore} aligns these labels to each sampled action chunk, padding with the last observed value when the chunk extends beyond the demonstration length.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Noise-Aware Collision-Shaping Loss}
\label{sec:loss}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

A direct collision penalty on the SDF predictions would be problematic in the flow-matching setting: at high noise levels (large $t$), the noisy actions $x_t$ bear little resemblance to the clean trajectory, and the model's internal features primarily reflect noise rather than meaningful geometric structure. Penalizing collisions uniformly across all noise levels would inject noisy gradients that destabilize training. We address this with a noise-aware weighting scheme that activates the collision penalty only when the model operates on near-clean inputs.

\paragraph{Noise-aware weight.} We define a time-dependent weight
\begin{equation}
w(t) = \sigma\!\left(\alpha \, (t_0 - t)\right),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function, $t_0 = 0.5$ is the transition center, and $\alpha = 10$ controls the sharpness. When $t \ll t_0$ (low noise), $w(t) \approx 1$ and the collision penalty is fully active. When $t \gg t_0$ (high noise), $w(t) \approx 0$ and the penalty is suppressed. The sigmoid provides a smooth transition that avoids discontinuities in the loss landscape.

\paragraph{SDF regression loss.} The first auxiliary loss encourages the SDF head to accurately predict signed distances from the model's internal features:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} \left(\hat{s}_{i,j} - s_{i,j}\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of valid sample-timestep pairs with available ground-truth labels, $\hat{s}_{i,j}$ is the predicted signed distance, and $s_{i,j}$ is the ground truth. This loss ensures the auxiliary head provides a faithful geometric probe of the model's representations, which in turn supplies meaningful gradients for the collision penalty.

\paragraph{Collision penalty loss.} The second auxiliary loss penalizes predictions that fall below the safety margin $\delta$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} w(t_i) \cdot \mathrm{ReLU}\!\left(\delta - \hat{s}_{i,j}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\delta = 0.02\,\mathrm{m}$ (2\,cm safety margin) and $t_i$ is the flow-matching timestep for sample $i$. The ReLU activation ensures that only near-collision and penetrating states are penalized: when $\hat{s}_{i,j} > \delta$, the contribution is zero; when $\hat{s}_{i,j} < \delta$, the penalty grows linearly with the depth of violation. Combined with the noise-aware weight $w(t_i)$, this loss selectively shapes the velocity field in the low-noise regime where the model's action predictions are geometrically meaningful.

\paragraph{Total training objective.} The complete loss combines the flow-matching objective with both auxiliary terms:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{col}} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} = 0.1$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.01$. The relatively small weight on the collision term reflects its role as a shaping signal rather than a hard constraint: the primary training signal remains the flow-matching loss, and the collision penalty gently steers the learned velocity field toward safer regions without dominating the optimization.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Training Pipeline}
\label{sec:training}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\ours{} training follows the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ fine-tuning pipeline with minimal modifications. The base model is initialized from pretrained $\pi_{0.5}$ weights. We perform full fine-tuning of all parameters (not LoRA), as the 96\,GB VRAM per GPU provides sufficient capacity and full fine-tuning yields a stronger baseline for evaluating the incremental effect of collision shaping.

\paragraph{Gradient flow through the backbone.} A critical design choice is whether to allow gradients from the SDF losses to propagate back through the action expert. When $\hat{s} = \mathrm{SDFHead}(f_t)$ with $f_t$ treated as a leaf tensor (detached from the computation graph), the SDF losses train only the auxiliary head to predict distances accurately but do not influence action generation. To test our core hypothesis that collision-shaping can modify the velocity field itself, we enable gradient flow from the SDF head through the shared features $f_t$ into the action expert weights. Concretely, the feature tensor $f_t$ captured by the forward hook is used without detachment, so that $\nabla_{f_t} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}}$ and $\nabla_{f_t} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}}$ both contribute to the backbone parameter update. This is the mechanism by which safety awareness becomes embedded in the model's action generation process.

\paragraph{Optimization.} We use the AdamW optimizer with the same hyperparameters as the base $\pi_{0.5}$ configuration. The SDF head parameters are added to the same optimizer with shared learning rate and weight decay. Gradients from all parameters (base model and SDF head jointly) are clipped by global norm with $\mathrm{max\_norm} = 1.0$. For the 5,000-step training runs, we use a linear warmup of 1,500 steps. Training uses PyTorch Distributed Data Parallel (DDP) across multiple GPUs, with gradient averaging for both the base model and SDF head parameters. The per-GPU batch size is 64, yielding an effective batch size of 128 on 2 GPUs.

\paragraph{Data augmentation with SDF labels.} The training data pipeline wraps the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ data loader. The \texttt{SDFAugmentedDataset} class intercepts each training sample after all standard transforms (image preprocessing, action normalization, prompt construction) and appends the corresponding SDF labels by looking up the precomputed \texttt{SDFLabelStore} using the sample's task index, episode index, and frame index. Samples without matching SDF labels are marked as invalid and excluded from the SDF loss computation, ensuring that the flow-matching loss is unaffected by label coverage gaps. In total, SDF labels cover 57 tasks across 8 task suites: the four standard LIBERO suites (LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Goal, LIBERO-10) have full coverage (10/10 tasks each), while the four SafeLIBERO suites have partial coverage (4 to 5 tasks each). Each task provides 50 demonstrations with per-timestep signed distance annotations, yielding labels for approximately 75\% of all training tasks.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Inference}
\label{sec:inference}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

At inference time, the SDF auxiliary head is discarded entirely. The trained model is served using the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ inference pipeline, with the same checkpoint format and serving infrastructure. No SDF computation, distance queries, or collision checking is performed during deployment. The model generates actions by integrating its learned velocity field through the standard ODE solver, exactly as the unmodified $\pi_{0.5}$ would. The safety behavior manifests through the modified model weights: the collision-shaping training signal has adjusted the velocity field to naturally produce trajectories that maintain clearance from obstacles.

This property distinguishes \ours{} from inference-time safety methods such as runtime trajectory filtering, control barrier functions, or guided sampling. These approaches add computational overhead proportional to the number of denoising steps or constraint evaluations, and may require access to the scene geometry at deployment. \ours{} incurs zero additional latency, zero additional memory, and requires no geometric information at test time.

Optionally, the SDF head can be retained during inference as a passive safety monitor (verification mode), providing real-time signed distance estimates at minimal computational overhead without influencing action generation. This mode enables post-hoc collision risk assessment for safety-critical deployment scenarios.

\paragraph{Connection to guidance distillation.} Our approach bears a structural analogy to guidance distillation in diffusion models. In classifier-guided diffusion, an external classifier provides a gradient signal $\nabla_{x_t} \log p(y | x_t)$ that steers the sampling process toward a desired class $y$. Guidance distillation trains a single model to reproduce the effect of this guidance without requiring the classifier at inference. Similarly, \ours{} uses the SDF auxiliary head as a ``geometric safety classifier'' that provides a training-time gradient signal directing the velocity field away from collision regions. After training, the model has internalized this signal, and the auxiliary head (analogous to the classifier) is no longer needed. Unlike classical guidance distillation, which replicates a guided sampling trajectory during training, our method shapes the velocity field directly through auxiliary gradients. The SDF head provides a training-time geometric signal that modifies the learned flow rather than distilling a pre-computed guided process. A further difference is that our method operates in the flow-matching framework rather than score-based diffusion, and the guidance signal comes from geometric structure (signed distances) rather than semantic class labels.


%=============================================================================
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
%=============================================================================

We evaluate \ours{} on the SafeLIBERO benchmark and standard LIBERO tasks to answer four questions: (1) Does internalized collision awareness improve safety without sacrificing task success? (2) How does \ours{} compare to post-hoc safety methods that operate at inference time? (3) Which components of our training objective are essential? (4) How does the collision-shaping loss interact with the flow-matching training dynamics?

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Base model and training.} We build on $\pi_{0.5}$, the open-source flow-matching VLA released by Physical Intelligence via the openpi codebase. All experiments use full fine-tuning of the entire model (not LoRA adapters), as the available GPU memory permits this and full fine-tuning provides a stronger baseline for isolating the effect of collision shaping. Training runs for 5,000 gradient steps on two NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96\,GB VRAM each) using PyTorch Distributed Data Parallel (DDP). The per-GPU batch size is 64, yielding an effective batch size of 128. We use AdamW with a linear warmup of 1,500 steps to a peak learning rate of $5 \times 10^{-5}$, followed by cosine decay. Gradients from all parameters (base model and SDF head jointly) are clipped by global norm with $\mathrm{max\_norm} = 1.0$. The vanilla $\pi_{0.5}$ model is trained with the identical configuration but without the SDF auxiliary head or collision-shaping loss.

\paragraph{\ours{} configuration.} The SDF auxiliary head is a two-layer MLP ($1024 \to 256 \to 1$, approximately 263K parameters) attached to the action expert's output features via a forward hook. The total training loss combines the standard flow-matching objective with two auxiliary terms weighted by $\lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} = 0.1$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.01$. The collision penalty uses a safety margin $\delta = 2$\,cm and is gated by a noise-aware sigmoid weight $w(t) = \sigma(\alpha(t_0 - t))$ with transition center $t_0 = 0.5$ and sharpness $\alpha = 10$. Gradients from both SDF losses propagate through the shared backbone features (\texttt{sdf\_grad\_to\_backbone = True}), which is essential for the collision-shaping signal to influence action generation.

\paragraph{SDF ground-truth labels.} Per-timestep signed distance labels are precomputed offline from LIBERO demonstration data using MuJoCo's analytical geometry engine (\texttt{mj\_geomDistance}). For each demonstration timestep, the minimum signed distance between the robot's end-effector collision geometries and all scene objects (excluding currently grasped objects) is recorded. SDF labels cover all four standard LIBERO suites at full task coverage (10/10 tasks each) and partial coverage for the four SafeLIBERO suites (4 to 5 tasks each), yielding labels for approximately 75\% of all training tasks. Each task contributes 50 demonstrations with per-timestep annotations.

\paragraph{Benchmarks.} We evaluate on two benchmarks. SafeLIBERO \citep{hu2025aegis} consists of 32 collision scenarios across four suites that mirror the standard LIBERO \citep{liu2023libero} task categories: SafeLIBERO-Spatial (4 tasks), SafeLIBERO-Object (4 tasks), SafeLIBERO-Goal (4 tasks), and SafeLIBERO-Long (4 tasks with longer horizons). Each task includes two difficulty levels (Level I and Level II), yielding 32 collision scenarios in total. We additionally evaluate on standard LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks) to verify that collision awareness does not degrade manipulation performance on tasks without explicit collision hazards.

\paragraph{Metrics.} We report three metrics. Task Success Rate (TSR) measures the fraction of episodes in which the robot completes the instructed task. Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) measures the fraction of episodes free of any contact with designated obstacle objects. Safety Score (SS) is defined as $\mathrm{SS} = \mathrm{TSR} \times \mathrm{CAR}$ and captures the joint requirement that a policy must both complete tasks and avoid collisions. We target 3 random seeds per configuration; results in this version report seed=1 with 5 eval trials per task.

\paragraph{Baselines.} We compare against two methods on the SafeLIBERO benchmark. (1)~$\pi_{0.5}$ vanilla: the same base model trained with identical hyperparameters and data but without the SDF head or collision-shaping loss, serving as a controlled ablation of our method's contribution. (2)~AEGIS/VLSA \citep{hu2025aegis}: the current SafeLIBERO state-of-the-art, which applies a plug-and-play Control Barrier Function (CBF) safety layer on top of a trained VLA.

